-- An ROR on one side of a RAND. `b` records `x` at 10, `a` is fulfilled at
-- 20, and `c`, on the other side of the RAND, reads `x` at 30. L4 runs no
-- more of an ROR once an earlier alternative is fulfilled, so it never runs
-- `b`, and `c` cannot see its record; written `b` ROR `a`, it would run `b`
-- first and `c` would see it (ror-in-rand-swapped.l4). What `c` saw depends
-- on how the ROR is written, so evaluation stops with an error naming the
-- record, the read and the alternative L4 did not run. (smucclaw/l4-ide#1069)

DECLARE Party IS ONE OF P, Q, R
DECLARE Act IS ONE OF actA, actB, actZ, actR

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`a` MEANS
    PARTY P
    MUST actA
    WITHIN 100

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`b` MEANS
    PARTY Q
    MAY actB
    WITHIN 100
    HENCE COMMIT `x` IS TRUE
          HENCE `z`
    LEST FULFILLED

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`z` MEANS
    PARTY Q
    MUST actZ
    WITHIN 100

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`c` MEANS
    PARTY R
    MUST actR PROVIDED (RECALL OFFICIAL's `x`) EQUALS JUST TRUE
    WITHIN 40
    HENCE COMMIT `c saw x` IS TRUE HENCE FULFILLED

GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
`whole` MEANS (`a` ROR `b`) RAND `c`

#TRACE `whole` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY Q DOES actB AT 10
    PARTY P DOES actA AT 20
    PARTY R DOES actR AT 30
    PARTY P DOES actA AT 50
